    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.test\r\n\r\n### Question\r\n\r\nThis is a bit of a strange one to describe but I'll do my best. Essentially I have two functions using different implementations (one is using treesitter the other is using patterns) but they are returning the exact same thing. Currently I have the tests like this,\r\n```lua\r\nlocal expected = ... -- whatever\r\nlocal ret = child.lua([[ return func_re() ]])\r\neq(ret, expected)\r\nret = child.lua([[ return func_ts() ]])\r\neq(ret, expected)\r\n```\r\n\r\nIdeally I would like to be able to do this,\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal expected = ... -- whatever\r\nlocal ret = func()\r\neq(ret, expected)\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhere `func()` would change between `child.lua([[ return func_re() ]])` and `child.lua([[ return func_ts() ]])`. Is this possible to do in any way? I thought about using nested tests, but you can't have something specific to change per test. Or at least I haven't understood the docs well enough to know how to do something like this.",
    "bodyHTML": "<h3 dir=\"auto\">Contributing guidelines</h3>\n<ul class=\"contains-task-list\">\n<li class=\"task-list-item\"><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"\" disabled=\"\" class=\"task-list-item-checkbox\" aria-label=\"Completed task\" checked=\"\"> I have read <a href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\">CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Module(s)</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">mini.test</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Question</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This is a bit of a strange one to describe but I'll do my best. Essentially I have two functions using different implementations (one is using treesitter the other is using patterns) but they are returning the exact same thing. Currently I have the tests like this,</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local expected = ... -- whatever\nlocal ret = child.lua([[ return func_re() ]])\neq(ret, expected)\nret = child.lua([[ return func_ts() ]])\neq(ret, expected)\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">expected</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">...</span> <span class=\"pl-c\"><span class=\"pl-c\">--</span> whatever</span>\n<span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">ret</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">child</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">lua</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">[[</span> return func_re() <span class=\"pl-pds\">]]</span></span>)\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">eq</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">ret</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">expected</span>)\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">ret</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">child</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">lua</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">[[</span> return func_ts() <span class=\"pl-pds\">]]</span></span>)\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">eq</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">ret</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">expected</span>)</pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Ideally I would like to be able to do this,</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local expected = ... -- whatever\nlocal ret = func()\neq(ret, expected)\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">expected</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">...</span> <span class=\"pl-c\"><span class=\"pl-c\">--</span> whatever</span>\n<span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">ret</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">func</span>()\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">eq</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">ret</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">expected</span>)</pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">where <code class=\"notranslate\">func()</code> would change between <code class=\"notranslate\">child.lua([[ return func_re() ]])</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">child.lua([[ return func_ts() ]])</code>. Is this possible to do in any way? I thought about using nested tests, but you can't have something specific to change per test. Or at least I haven't understood the docs well enough to know how to do something like this.</p>",
    "bodyText": "Contributing guidelines\n\n I have read CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\n\nModule(s)\nmini.test\nQuestion\nThis is a bit of a strange one to describe but I'll do my best. Essentially I have two functions using different implementations (one is using treesitter the other is using patterns) but they are returning the exact same thing. Currently I have the tests like this,\nlocal expected = ... -- whatever\nlocal ret = child.lua([[ return func_re() ]])\neq(ret, expected)\nret = child.lua([[ return func_ts() ]])\neq(ret, expected)\nIdeally I would like to be able to do this,\nlocal expected = ... -- whatever\nlocal ret = func()\neq(ret, expected)\nwhere func() would change between child.lua([[ return func_re() ]]) and child.lua([[ return func_ts() ]]). Is this possible to do in any way? I thought about using nested tests, but you can't have something specific to change per test. Or at least I haven't understood the docs well enough to know how to do something like this.",
